\[\alpha\left\|\,w^{\varepsilon}\right\|_{X}^{2}\leqslant C_{1}\varepsilon^{2} \left\|\,w^{\varepsilon}\right\|_{X}^{2}+C_{2}\varepsilon^{N+1}\left\|\,\dot{ \lambda}_{N}\right\|_{M}\left\|\,w^{\varepsilon}\right\|_{X}+C_{3}\varepsilon ^{2N+2}\left\|\,\dot{\lambda}_{N}\right\|_{M}^{2}\]

which implies for \(\varepsilon\leqslant\varepsilon_{0}\) small enough

(4.39) \[\left\|\,w^{\varepsilon}\right\|_{X}\leqslant C_{4}\varepsilon^{N+1}\left\|\, \dot{\lambda}_{N}\right\|_{M}.\]

Now, it follows from (4.35) and Corollary 4.1 that

(4.40) \[\left\|\,\dot{\lambda}_{N}\right\|_{M}\leqslant C_{5}\left\|\,\dot{\lambda} \right\|_{M}\leqslant C_{6}(\left\|\,l\right\|_{X^{\prime}}+\left\|\,\dot{ \gamma}\right\|_{M^{\prime}}),\]

with constants \(C_{5}\) and \(C_{6}\) of the form \(C^{N}\). Thus the desired inequality (4.36) is a consequence of (4.38), (4.39) and (4.40). 

